phoon -- show the PHase of the mOON    phoon  [ -l <lines> ]  [ <year> <month> <day> [ <hour> [ <minute> ] ] ]phoon draws the phase of the moon as a little picture.  Unlike programsthat just tell you how long since first quarter, phoon shows you the phase,lit from the correct side, with the days and hours to the next quarterbeside it.With no argument it draws the moon for right now.  Give a date -- as plainnumbers, year month day, and optionally an hour and minute on the 24-hourclock -- to see the moon then:    phoon                    tonight's moon, 23 lines tall    phoon -l 29              tonight's moon, larger    phoon 2025 12 25         the moon on Christmas Day 2025, at noon    phoon 2025 12 25 21 30   the same evening at 21:30Only a few sizes -- 18, 19, 23, 24 and 29 lines -- have a drawn face; othersizes fill the lit part with `@'.  Once in a while the moon turns out to bemade of green cheese.The phase is worked out from a simple approximation, accurate to a day orso, which is plenty for a picture.About this port    This is Jef Poskanzer's phoon of 1987 (comp.sources.unix volume 8),    carrying his own permission to use, copy, modify and distribute.  The    original leaned on his libtws date library, whose heart was a lex-built    parser for free-form English dates.  This build keeps just the picture:    a small tws.c supplies the current time and the arithmetic, and the date    is read as the numbers above, so no lex parser is needed.  The full    original sources are in SRC/phoon/ORIG.